1
00:00:00,140 --> 00:00:02,703
On November 21, 1915,

2
00:00:03,043 --> 00:00:04,564
a ship called Endurance

3
00:00:04,845 --> 00:00:06,166
sank in the Weddell Sea,

4
00:00:06,366 --> 00:00:07,247
near Antarctica.

5
00:00:07,787 --> 00:00:08,988
All twenty-eight men

6
00:00:09,049 --> 00:00:09,949
of her expedition

7
00:00:10,150 --> 00:00:11,151
were still alive.

8
00:00:11,611 --> 00:00:12,692
But they had no ship,

9
00:00:13,132 --> 00:00:14,914
no radio, and no way

10
00:00:14,954 --> 00:00:16,295
to tell the outside world

11
00:00:16,395 --> 00:00:17,036
where they were.

12
00:00:17,617 --> 00:00:18,497
They were stranded

13
00:00:18,578 --> 00:00:19,870
on drifting sea ice,

14
00:00:20,150 --> 00:00:21,823
with three small lifeboats

15
00:00:22,063 --> 00:00:23,124
and whatever supplies

16
00:00:23,184 --> 00:00:24,425
they had managed to save.

17
00:00:24,945 --> 00:00:26,147
To get everyone home,

18
00:00:26,567 --> 00:00:27,948
six men would eventually

19
00:00:28,008 --> 00:00:28,889
have to cross more

20
00:00:28,909 --> 00:00:30,410
than eight hundred miles

21
00:00:30,470 --> 00:00:32,772
of open ocean in a boat barely

22
00:00:32,953 --> 00:00:34,094
seven metres long.

23
00:00:34,654 --> 00:00:35,595
The other twenty-two

24
00:00:35,835 --> 00:00:36,596
would have to wait,

25
00:00:36,976 --> 00:00:38,057
without knowing whether

26
00:00:38,117 --> 00:00:39,659
those six had survived.

27
00:00:40,190 --> 00:00:41,282
And even if the boat

28
00:00:41,382 --> 00:00:42,824
reached help, someone

29
00:00:42,884 --> 00:00:44,445
would still have to find a way

30
00:00:44,585 --> 00:00:46,727
back through the ice.

31
00:00:46,867 --> 00:00:49,149
In 1914, Ernest Shackleton

32
00:00:49,249 --> 00:00:50,571
set out to lead the first

33
00:00:50,711 --> 00:00:52,432
expedition across Antarctica,

34
00:00:52,692 --> 00:00:53,954
travelling from one coast

35
00:00:53,994 --> 00:00:55,095
to the other by way

36
00:00:55,135 --> 00:00:56,075
of the South Pole.

37
00:00:56,616 --> 00:00:57,156
His ship,

38
00:00:57,457 --> 00:00:58,878
Endurance, was supposed

39
00:00:58,918 --> 00:01:00,070
to carry the expedition

40
00:01:00,210 --> 00:01:01,282
into the Weddell Sea,

41
00:01:01,522 --> 00:01:02,243
where the crossing

42
00:01:02,303 --> 00:01:02,844
would begin.

43
00:01:03,384 --> 00:01:05,426
But in January 1915,

44
00:01:05,766 --> 00:01:06,807
moving pack ice

45
00:01:06,947 --> 00:01:08,228
closed around the vessel.

46
00:01:08,749 --> 00:01:09,349
For months,

47
00:01:09,670 --> 00:01:10,951
Captain Frank Worsley

48
00:01:11,051 --> 00:01:12,052
tracked their position

49
00:01:12,232 --> 00:01:13,433
while the crew maintained

50
00:01:13,453 --> 00:01:14,594
the ship and waited

51
00:01:14,654 --> 00:01:15,795
for a chance to escape.

52
00:01:16,356 --> 00:01:17,577
The sea ice moved

53
00:01:17,617 --> 00:01:18,938
with the winds and currents.

54
00:01:19,409 --> 00:01:20,430
Instead of carrying them

55
00:01:20,510 --> 00:01:21,863
toward their destination,

56
00:01:22,143 --> 00:01:23,364
it took the trapped ship

57
00:01:23,544 --> 00:01:24,645
wherever it drifted.

58
00:01:25,226 --> 00:01:26,507
Then, in October,

59
00:01:26,767 --> 00:01:27,328
the pressure

60
00:01:27,408 --> 00:01:28,449
became too great.

61
00:01:28,849 --> 00:01:30,130
The wooden hull began

62
00:01:30,170 --> 00:01:30,851
to give way.

63
00:01:31,351 --> 00:01:32,152
Water entered,

64
00:01:32,492 --> 00:01:33,493
and on October 27,

65
00:01:33,553 --> 00:01:35,595
Shackleton ordered the men

66
00:01:35,715 --> 00:01:36,796
to abandon ship.

67
00:01:37,297 --> 00:01:38,798
They carried three lifeboats,

68
00:01:39,078 --> 00:01:40,470
provisions and equipment

69
00:01:40,751 --> 00:01:41,903
onto the surrounding ice.

70
00:01:42,543 --> 00:01:43,784
On November 21,

71
00:01:44,265 --> 00:01:45,546
Endurance sank.

72
00:01:46,087 --> 00:01:46,907
The expedition

73
00:01:46,967 --> 00:01:47,968
had never reached

74
00:01:48,028 --> 00:01:48,869
its starting point

75
00:01:48,949 --> 00:01:50,190
on the Antarctic continent.

76
00:01:50,771 --> 00:01:52,512
Now the men had to find

77
00:01:52,672 --> 00:01:53,673
another way home.

78
00:01:54,774 --> 00:01:56,055
Shackleton first tried

79
00:01:56,135 --> 00:01:57,777
moving toward land on foot.

80
00:01:58,137 --> 00:01:59,690
But hauling heavy lifeboats

81
00:01:59,750 --> 00:02:00,831
across broken ice

82
00:02:00,962 --> 00:02:02,503
proved almost impossible.

83
00:02:03,044 --> 00:02:04,425
Ridges blocked their path,

84
00:02:04,805 --> 00:02:06,227
cracks forced detours,

85
00:02:06,527 --> 00:02:07,047
and the men

86
00:02:07,127 --> 00:02:08,288
made little progress.

87
00:02:08,849 --> 00:02:09,990
They abandoned the march

88
00:02:10,230 --> 00:02:11,351
and established a camp

89
00:02:11,431 --> 00:02:12,472
on a drifting floe.

90
00:02:13,053 --> 00:02:13,673
For months,

91
00:02:13,994 --> 00:02:15,395
they waited as the current

92
00:02:15,475 --> 00:02:16,276
carried them north.

93
00:02:16,776 --> 00:02:17,937
They rationed their food,

94
00:02:18,237 --> 00:02:19,810
hunted seals and penguins,

95
00:02:20,090 --> 00:02:21,522
and kept the lifeboats ready.

96
00:02:22,163 --> 00:02:23,885
In April 1916,

97
00:02:24,145 --> 00:02:25,186
the ice beneath them

98
00:02:25,286 --> 00:02:26,547
began breaking apart.

99
00:02:27,067 --> 00:02:28,669
On April 9, they launched

100
00:02:28,709 --> 00:02:30,010
their boats into the water.

101
00:02:30,550 --> 00:02:31,531
For nearly a week,

102
00:02:31,772 --> 00:02:33,433
the crews rowed and sailed

103
00:02:33,493 --> 00:02:34,774
between moving floes,

104
00:02:35,135 --> 00:02:36,115
struggling to reach

105
00:02:36,296 --> 00:02:37,096
Elephant Island.

106
00:02:37,657 --> 00:02:38,618
On April 15,

107
00:02:39,058 --> 00:02:40,230
all twenty-eight men

108
00:02:40,490 --> 00:02:41,382
finally landed.

109
00:02:41,723 --> 00:02:42,403
They had reached

110
00:02:42,523 --> 00:02:43,384
solid ground,

111
00:02:43,764 --> 00:02:44,865
but Elephant Island

112
00:02:44,945 --> 00:02:46,627
was uninhabited and far

113
00:02:46,727 --> 00:02:47,948
from normal shipping routes.

114
00:02:48,509 --> 00:02:49,810
No rescue ship knew

115
00:02:49,870 --> 00:02:50,470
they were there.

116
00:02:51,131 --> 00:02:52,152
Shackleton decided

117
00:02:52,212 --> 00:02:53,974
that a small crew must leave

118
00:02:54,034 --> 00:02:54,754
to find help.

119
00:02:55,155 --> 00:02:56,075
Their destination

120
00:02:56,176 --> 00:02:57,256
was South Georgia,

121
00:02:57,617 --> 00:02:59,866
roughly 1,300 kilometres —

122
00:03:00,490 --> 00:03:01,970
over 800 miles —

123
00:03:02,023 --> 00:03:02,844
to the northeast,

124
00:03:03,164 --> 00:03:04,545
across the Scotia Sea.

125
00:03:04,966 --> 00:03:06,187
There were whaling stations

126
00:03:06,267 --> 00:03:06,847
on the island.

127
00:03:07,308 --> 00:03:08,489
If the men could reach one,

128
00:03:08,749 --> 00:03:09,429
they could obtain

129
00:03:09,490 --> 00:03:11,171
help and organise a rescue.

130
00:03:11,651 --> 00:03:12,692
Their only suitable

131
00:03:12,772 --> 00:03:14,074
vessel was the largest

132
00:03:14,174 --> 00:03:16,015
lifeboat, the James Caird.

133
00:03:16,436 --> 00:03:17,096
The carpenter,

134
00:03:17,236 --> 00:03:18,618
Harry McNish, raised

135
00:03:18,678 --> 00:03:20,430
its sides, fitted a partial

136
00:03:20,490 --> 00:03:21,963
covering of wood and canvas,

137
00:03:22,303 --> 00:03:23,184
and worked to make

138
00:03:23,224 --> 00:03:24,605
the hull more watertight.

139
00:03:25,146 --> 00:03:26,787
Shackleton initially intended

140
00:03:26,847 --> 00:03:28,108
to leave Thomas Crean

141
00:03:28,188 --> 00:03:29,870
on Elephant Island to assist

142
00:03:29,930 --> 00:03:31,051
his second-in-command,

143
00:03:31,231 --> 00:03:31,992
Frank Wild.

144
00:03:32,332 --> 00:03:33,793
But after discussing the plan,

145
00:03:34,074 --> 00:03:35,075
he decided to take

146
00:03:35,175 --> 00:03:36,095
Crean on the ocean

147
00:03:36,155 --> 00:03:37,076
crossing instead.

148
00:03:37,657 --> 00:03:39,639
On April 24, Shackleton

149
00:03:39,670 --> 00:03:40,931
departed with Worsley,

150
00:03:41,162 --> 00:03:42,463
McNish, Crean,

151
00:03:42,724 --> 00:03:43,684
Timothy McCarthy

152
00:03:43,925 --> 00:03:44,905
and John Vincent.

153
00:03:45,386 --> 00:03:46,907
Twenty-two men remained

154
00:03:47,067 --> 00:03:48,268
under Wild's command.

155
00:03:48,869 --> 00:03:50,150
Wild's first challenge

156
00:03:50,230 --> 00:03:51,371
was finding shelter.

157
00:03:51,852 --> 00:03:52,933
An ice cave offered

158
00:03:52,993 --> 00:03:54,134
protection from the wind,

159
00:03:54,434 --> 00:03:55,215
but it was wet

160
00:03:55,435 --> 00:03:56,876
and unsuitable for living in.

161
00:03:57,357 --> 00:03:58,598
The men abandoned it.

162
00:03:59,078 --> 00:04:00,611
Instead, they built low

163
00:04:00,711 --> 00:04:02,503
stone walls and used two

164
00:04:02,703 --> 00:04:03,824
overturned lifeboats,

165
00:04:04,145 --> 00:04:05,626
canvas and other salvaged

166
00:04:05,666 --> 00:04:07,187
materials to form a roof.

167
00:04:07,668 --> 00:04:08,929
The shelter was cramped,

168
00:04:09,189 --> 00:04:09,690
but it gave

169
00:04:09,730 --> 00:04:10,550
the men somewhere

170
00:04:10,591 --> 00:04:12,292
to live as winter approached.

171
00:04:12,772 --> 00:04:14,334
While Wild organised life

172
00:04:14,394 --> 00:04:16,236
inside it, the James Caird

173
00:04:16,296 --> 00:04:18,237
was already far out at sea.

174
00:04:18,858 --> 00:04:20,050
Finding South Georgia

175
00:04:20,130 --> 00:04:20,891
required more

176
00:04:20,942 --> 00:04:22,183
than sailing northeast.

177
00:04:22,623 --> 00:04:23,424
It was a small

178
00:04:23,644 --> 00:04:25,446
island in an enormous ocean.

179
00:04:25,926 --> 00:04:27,548
If the James Caird passed too

180
00:04:27,628 --> 00:04:29,470
far to either side, the men

181
00:04:29,550 --> 00:04:30,851
might miss it entirely.

182
00:04:31,331 --> 00:04:32,632
Worsley had to navigate

183
00:04:32,692 --> 00:04:33,493
with a sextant,

184
00:04:33,793 --> 00:04:35,495
a compass and calculations

185
00:04:35,535 --> 00:04:36,936
of their estimated progress.

186
00:04:37,397 --> 00:04:38,357
He needed the sun

187
00:04:38,418 --> 00:04:39,650
to determine their position.

188
00:04:40,090 --> 00:04:40,991
But cloud cover,

189
00:04:41,222 --> 00:04:42,944
heavy seas and the constant

190
00:04:42,984 --> 00:04:43,845
movement of the boat

191
00:04:44,085 --> 00:04:45,666
made observations difficult.

192
00:04:46,087 --> 00:04:46,967
During the crossing,

193
00:04:47,187 --> 00:04:47,728
he obtained

194
00:04:47,828 --> 00:04:49,810
only four usable observations

195
00:04:49,850 --> 00:04:50,370
of the sun.

196
00:04:50,791 --> 00:04:51,371
Between them,

197
00:04:51,611 --> 00:04:52,953
he estimated their position

198
00:04:53,033 --> 00:04:54,154
from the course steered

199
00:04:54,294 --> 00:04:55,355
and distance travelled,

200
00:04:55,675 --> 00:04:56,856
adjusting whenever new

201
00:04:56,956 --> 00:04:57,797
evidence allowed.

202
00:04:58,257 --> 00:04:59,438
The others kept the boat

203
00:04:59,510 --> 00:05:00,530
moving and afloat.

204
00:05:00,942 --> 00:05:02,003
They worked in watches,

205
00:05:02,323 --> 00:05:03,284
handling the sails,

206
00:05:03,624 --> 00:05:05,206
steering and bailing water.

207
00:05:05,746 --> 00:05:06,687
Ice accumulated

208
00:05:06,747 --> 00:05:08,248
on the vessel, adding weight

209
00:05:08,288 --> 00:05:09,530
that had to be removed.

210
00:05:10,050 --> 00:05:11,812
After nearly two weeks at sea,

211
00:05:12,072 --> 00:05:12,913
they were approaching

212
00:05:12,953 --> 00:05:14,334
where Worsley calculated

213
00:05:14,434 --> 00:05:15,675
South Georgia should be.

214
00:05:16,216 --> 00:05:17,657
On May 8, land

215
00:05:17,697 --> 00:05:18,758
appeared through a break

216
00:05:18,798 --> 00:05:19,458
in the clouds.

217
00:05:19,930 --> 00:05:21,382
They had found the island.

218
00:05:21,683 --> 00:05:22,744
But heavy seas

219
00:05:22,844 --> 00:05:24,005
and dangerous surf

220
00:05:24,145 --> 00:05:24,685
prevented

221
00:05:24,765 --> 00:05:25,806
an immediate landing.

222
00:05:26,227 --> 00:05:27,348
For two more days,

223
00:05:27,428 --> 00:05:28,028
they remained

224
00:05:28,108 --> 00:05:29,409
offshore, searching

225
00:05:29,490 --> 00:05:30,510
for a safe approach.

226
00:05:31,071 --> 00:05:31,812
On May 10,

227
00:05:32,132 --> 00:05:33,473
the six men finally

228
00:05:33,533 --> 00:05:35,155
landed in King Haakon Bay,

229
00:05:35,455 --> 00:05:36,396
on South Georgia's

230
00:05:36,476 --> 00:05:37,276
western coast.

231
00:05:37,737 --> 00:05:38,738
The whaling stations

232
00:05:38,798 --> 00:05:39,470
were on the other

233
00:05:39,550 --> 00:05:40,410
side of the island.

234
00:05:40,862 --> 00:05:41,963
With the James Caird

235
00:05:42,003 --> 00:05:42,623
and its crew

236
00:05:42,683 --> 00:05:44,085
in their exhausted condition,

237
00:05:44,405 --> 00:05:45,306
attempting to reach

238
00:05:45,346 --> 00:05:46,667
those stations by sailing

239
00:05:46,727 --> 00:05:47,528
around the coast

240
00:05:47,728 --> 00:05:48,749
was too dangerous.

241
00:05:49,289 --> 00:05:50,871
Shackleton decided to cross

242
00:05:50,931 --> 00:05:52,152
the mountains instead.

243
00:05:52,632 --> 00:05:53,893
McNish, Vincent

244
00:05:54,014 --> 00:05:54,714
and McCarthy

245
00:05:54,934 --> 00:05:55,815
remained behind.

246
00:05:56,396 --> 00:05:57,657
Shackleton, Worsley

247
00:05:57,737 --> 00:05:59,378
and Crean set out on foot

248
00:05:59,570 --> 00:06:00,530
on May 19.

249
00:06:00,991 --> 00:06:02,103
There was no established

250
00:06:02,203 --> 00:06:03,224
route through the mountains

251
00:06:03,304 --> 00:06:03,905
and glaciers.

252
00:06:04,405 --> 00:06:06,407
At one steep icy descent,

253
00:06:06,707 --> 00:06:08,048
Worsley and Crean held

254
00:06:08,088 --> 00:06:09,510
the rope while Shackleton

255
00:06:09,610 --> 00:06:10,951
used a carpenter's adze

256
00:06:11,011 --> 00:06:12,532
to cut steps into the ice.

257
00:06:12,993 --> 00:06:13,934
Descending roughly

258
00:06:14,094 --> 00:06:15,475
five hundred feet took

259
00:06:15,515 --> 00:06:16,696
them about two hours.

260
00:06:17,196 --> 00:06:18,498
Then the three continued

261
00:06:18,558 --> 00:06:20,170
across snowfields and ridges,

262
00:06:20,550 --> 00:06:21,743
searching for a passage

263
00:06:21,823 --> 00:06:23,204
toward the inhabited coast.

264
00:06:23,704 --> 00:06:24,585
On May 20,

265
00:06:24,905 --> 00:06:25,846
after approximately

266
00:06:25,986 --> 00:06:27,708
thirty-six hours of travelling,

267
00:06:27,948 --> 00:06:28,949
they reached the whaling

268
00:06:29,009 --> 00:06:30,250
station at Stromness.

269
00:06:30,731 --> 00:06:31,752
The three men left

270
00:06:31,852 --> 00:06:32,772
on South Georgia's

271
00:06:32,833 --> 00:06:33,633
western shore

272
00:06:33,853 --> 00:06:34,734
were subsequently

273
00:06:34,794 --> 00:06:35,655
brought to safety.

274
00:06:36,095 --> 00:06:36,956
But no news

275
00:06:37,016 --> 00:06:38,518
of any of this had reached

276
00:06:38,658 --> 00:06:39,358
Elephant Island.

277
00:06:39,910 --> 00:06:40,931
Wild and the other

278
00:06:41,022 --> 00:06:41,883
twenty-one men

279
00:06:42,103 --> 00:06:43,104
were still waiting.

280
00:06:43,664 --> 00:06:44,725
Inside their shelter,

281
00:06:44,925 --> 00:06:46,107
they maintained a daily

282
00:06:46,167 --> 00:06:47,728
routine, gathered food

283
00:06:47,788 --> 00:06:49,269
when they could and endured

284
00:06:49,309 --> 00:06:50,470
the Antarctic winter

285
00:06:50,550 --> 00:06:51,591
without knowing whether

286
00:06:51,631 --> 00:06:53,113
the James Caird had reached

287
00:06:53,173 --> 00:06:54,014
its destination.

288
00:06:54,514 --> 00:06:55,215
Wild kept

289
00:06:55,255 --> 00:06:56,296
the group organised.

290
00:06:56,736 --> 00:06:57,797
Each day, he rolled

291
00:06:57,857 --> 00:06:59,198
up his own sleeping bag,

292
00:06:59,450 --> 00:07:00,711
repeating that Shackleton

293
00:07:00,842 --> 00:07:02,063
might arrive that day.

294
00:07:02,523 --> 00:07:03,584
The others continued

295
00:07:03,624 --> 00:07:04,685
the work of maintaining

296
00:07:04,725 --> 00:07:06,267
the camp and finding food.

297
00:07:06,767 --> 00:07:08,789
Days passed. Then weeks.

298
00:07:09,409 --> 00:07:10,771
Shackleton's first rescue

299
00:07:10,811 --> 00:07:12,132
attempt, aboard the whaling

300
00:07:12,172 --> 00:07:13,313
ship Southern Sky,

301
00:07:13,553 --> 00:07:14,954
was stopped by pack ice.

302
00:07:15,395 --> 00:07:16,196
The next attempt,

303
00:07:16,496 --> 00:07:17,857
using the Uruguayan vessel

304
00:07:18,037 --> 00:07:19,850
Instituto de Pesca No. 1,

305
00:07:20,090 --> 00:07:21,051
and a later voyage

306
00:07:21,082 --> 00:07:21,863
aboard the schooner

307
00:07:22,023 --> 00:07:23,344
Emma also failed

308
00:07:23,384 --> 00:07:24,645
to reach Elephant Island.

309
00:07:25,226 --> 00:07:26,687
Three attempts had failed,

310
00:07:26,987 --> 00:07:28,108
and the men on the island

311
00:07:28,248 --> 00:07:29,590
still had no news.

312
00:07:30,130 --> 00:07:31,852
As winter continued, food

313
00:07:31,912 --> 00:07:33,493
became increasingly scarce.

314
00:07:33,934 --> 00:07:35,375
By late August, the men

315
00:07:35,415 --> 00:07:36,676
were using what remained

316
00:07:36,896 --> 00:07:37,957
and gathering limpets

317
00:07:38,017 --> 00:07:39,398
and seaweed along the shore.

318
00:07:39,950 --> 00:07:40,991
Shackleton secured

319
00:07:41,002 --> 00:07:42,303
a fourth opportunity.

320
00:07:42,744 --> 00:07:43,804
The Chilean government

321
00:07:43,865 --> 00:07:45,666
provided Yelcho, a small

322
00:07:45,746 --> 00:07:46,867
steam tug commanded

323
00:07:46,927 --> 00:07:48,889
by naval officer Luis Pardo.

324
00:07:49,309 --> 00:07:50,631
It was not an icebreaker.

325
00:07:51,031 --> 00:07:52,252
The ship could not force

326
00:07:52,312 --> 00:07:53,493
its way through a solid

327
00:07:53,553 --> 00:07:54,694
barrier of pack ice.

328
00:07:55,235 --> 00:07:56,396
On August 25,

329
00:07:56,716 --> 00:07:57,637
Yelcho departed

330
00:07:57,797 --> 00:07:58,958
with Shackleton aboard.

331
00:07:59,269 --> 00:08:00,090
As the pack ice

332
00:08:00,190 --> 00:08:01,623
shifted toward the northeast,

333
00:08:02,003 --> 00:08:02,884
Shackleton chose

334
00:08:02,924 --> 00:08:04,405
to approach Elephant Island

335
00:08:04,505 --> 00:08:05,486
from the northwest.

336
00:08:05,966 --> 00:08:07,308
Pardo then had to bring

337
00:08:07,348 --> 00:08:08,369
the vessel safely

338
00:08:08,409 --> 00:08:09,189
through the waters

339
00:08:09,269 --> 00:08:10,010
near the island.

340
00:08:10,490 --> 00:08:12,212
In fog, he navigated

341
00:08:12,272 --> 00:08:13,633
among numerous grounded

342
00:08:13,773 --> 00:08:15,595
icebergs, guiding the small

343
00:08:15,675 --> 00:08:16,816
tug toward the camp.

344
00:08:17,437 --> 00:08:19,378
On August 30, Yelcho

345
00:08:19,470 --> 00:08:20,711
reached Elephant Island.

346
00:08:21,202 --> 00:08:21,963
The men ashore

347
00:08:22,043 --> 00:08:22,623
were preparing

348
00:08:22,784 --> 00:08:24,385
food when a cry announced

349
00:08:24,425 --> 00:08:25,366
the approaching ship.

350
00:08:25,866 --> 00:08:28,050
At first, the words “Ship O!”

351
00:08:28,349 --> 00:08:30,500
were mistaken for “Lunch O!”

352
00:08:30,971 --> 00:08:33,033
Then the men saw the vessel.

353
00:08:33,573 --> 00:08:34,674
After more than four

354
00:08:34,834 --> 00:08:36,896
months of isolation, a ship

355
00:08:37,016 --> 00:08:38,157
had reached their shore.

356
00:08:38,618 --> 00:08:40,130
Pardo brought Yelcho close

357
00:08:40,170 --> 00:08:41,451
enough for the evacuation

358
00:08:41,502 --> 00:08:42,003
to begin.

359
00:08:42,523 --> 00:08:43,684
All twenty-two men

360
00:08:43,744 --> 00:08:44,485
who had remained

361
00:08:44,565 --> 00:08:45,506
on Elephant Island

362
00:08:45,686 --> 00:08:46,607
were taken aboard.

363
00:08:47,147 --> 00:08:49,069
Every one of them was alive.

364
00:08:49,710 --> 00:08:50,971
The wider Imperial

365
00:08:51,051 --> 00:08:52,712
Trans-Antarctic Expedition

366
00:08:53,013 --> 00:08:54,134
had a different outcome.

367
00:08:54,654 --> 00:08:55,335
On the opposite

368
00:08:55,395 --> 00:08:56,396
side of Antarctica,

369
00:08:56,676 --> 00:08:58,297
the separate Ross Sea Party

370
00:08:58,377 --> 00:08:59,559
suffered its own ordeal,

371
00:08:59,930 --> 00:09:01,322
and three men died.

372
00:09:01,843 --> 00:09:02,343
The planned

373
00:09:02,423 --> 00:09:03,685
crossing of Antarctica

374
00:09:03,945 --> 00:09:05,066
was never completed.

375
00:09:05,526 --> 00:09:06,708
But the twenty-two men

376
00:09:06,808 --> 00:09:07,228
rescued

377
00:09:07,268 --> 00:09:08,269
from Elephant Island,

378
00:09:08,549 --> 00:09:09,630
together with the six

379
00:09:09,670 --> 00:09:10,751
who had sailed for help,

380
00:09:11,072 --> 00:09:12,633
accounted for every member

381
00:09:12,693 --> 00:09:13,834
of the Endurance party.

382
00:09:14,335 --> 00:09:16,156
The expedition lost its ship.

383
00:09:16,757 --> 00:09:17,958
All twenty-eight men

384
00:09:18,319 --> 00:09:19,560
came home alive.
